<h2>Where the news comes from</h2>
<p>Every day a program gathers what is new from public sources: PubMed, Europe PMC, bioRxiv and medRxiv preprints, the Nature, Science and Cell RSS feeds (title, authors, date and link only) and headlines from SINC, The Conversation and ScienceDaily. Nobody hand-picks what goes into «Latest» or «Nature · Science · Cell». «Classics» are the exception: the site proposes them and only those Sofía approves are published. <a href="{{ url('sources') }}">Every source and its licence</a>.</p>

<h2>The three signals of every study</h2>
<p>Always visible, on the card and on the study page. When a piece of data does not exist the signal says «no data»: we never guess.</p>
<dl class="method-signals">
  <dt>Review</dt>
  <dd><strong>Preprint</strong>: it comes from bioRxiv or medRxiv and has not been peer reviewed yet. <strong>Published in a journal</strong>: it appears in a journal. Almost every scientific journal peer reviews what it publishes, but this signal does not check it: it only says where the study was published. {{ m.gap("journal_name") }}</dd>
  <dt>Subjects</dt>
  <dd><strong>Humans</strong>, <strong>animals</strong> or both, from the MeSH descriptors added by the US National Library of Medicine. Newly published studies do not have them yet, and then the signal says «no data».</dd>
  <dt>Notices</dt>
  <dd><strong>Retracted</strong>, <strong>corrected</strong> or <strong>expression of concern</strong>, from the notices publishers deposit in Crossref and from the Retraction Watch database, which Crossref publishes and which also records retractions publishers never deposit. The site checks both every day, so a retraction that arrives years later shows up too. If a retracted study is later reinstated, it shows as <strong>corrected</strong>, with the link to the notice. A preprint can also show as <strong>withdrawn by the authors</strong>: bioRxiv and medRxiv say so in their own data, not in Crossref, and it then leaves «Latest».</dd>
</dl>

<h2>How summaries are written</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Short facts are taken from the article's abstract, and each fact must be in the original.</li>
  <li>A language model writes the English text from those facts only, then the Spanish text.</li>
  <li>Automatic checks: every sentence cites its source; figures and names match it; never 8 or more words in a row copied; no overclaiming («cure», «proves that», «breakthrough»…); the study's caveats are kept; and the Spanish spelling is correct.</li>
  <li>A judge, a model from another family, rereads each summary. Without its approval nothing is published.</li>
  <li>If anything fails, the summary is not published: a <strong>fallback text</strong> built only from the data is shown instead (the kind of study, its subjects, the journal, the date and whether it is a preprint or has notices).</li>
</ol>
<p>We never publish abstracts or paragraphs of the articles: only our own summaries, with a link to the original and its licence.</p>

<h2>What we do not do</h2>
<p>SYNAPTICA is science communication. It gives no medical advice and recommends no treatments.</p>
